18836 ### `vmedefaultkill` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
18837 
18838 `dev_vme.c`, default arm: it announces with **`debug()`** and then calls `exit(1)`.
18839 
18840 *** THIS ONE IS WORSE THAN THE USUAL SHAPE, AND THE REASON IS THE DIAGNOSTIC LEVEL. *** Every other
18841 unimplemented-register kill in this queue announces with `fatal()`, which this fork has measured cannot be
18842 silenced by `-q` -- `fatal()` has no early-out and goes straight to `va_debug`. This one uses `debug()`,
18843 which does have one (`if ((quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0) return;`), so at default verbosity **the message
18844 is not printed at all and the emulator dies with no output explaining why.** A user sees the process
18845 vanish; a gate sees a non-zero exit and no diagnostic to grep. That makes it the hardest member of the
18846 class to DIAGNOSE, independent of how hard it is to trigger.
18847 
18848 `machine_mvme88k.c` installs it unconditionally (`vme addr=...`, `VME2_BASE`) on a machine this project
18849 actively boots -- the same constructor attaches the PCC2 the recent rounds are about. Probing a bus
18850 controller's register space is exactly what firmware does.
18851 
18852 Disposition: member 5 (FAULT) is **available** -- M88K is one of the two families `memory_rw.c` delivers a
18853 failed-device exception for -- so `return 0` gives the guest the bus error real VME hardware would. Member
18854 3 is the alternative and would need the announce **promoted from `debug()` to `fatal()`** to be worth
18855 anything, which is a second change the round must make deliberately rather than inherit.
18856 
18857 ### `fbctrlportkill` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
18858 
18859 `dev_fbctrl.c`: **the PORT arm accepts an out-of-range port number with a warning and STORES it, and the
18860 DATA arm then calls `exit(1)` on that same stored value.** A two-step, guest-driven host kill in which the
18861 first step looks handled:
18862 
18863 ```c
18864 case DEV_FBCTRL_PORT:  d->current_port = idata;                 /* stored FIRST */
18865                        if (idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS)
18866                                fatal("[ WARNING: ... out of range! ]\n");   /* warned SECOND */
18867 case DEV_FBCTRL_DATA:  if (d->current_port < 0 || d->current_port >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS) {
18868                                fatal(...); exit(1); }
18869 ```
18870 
